            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Overview: Why Was the Second Appeal Rejected?</h2>
<p>An anonymized B2B productivity app was running Google Ads campaigns in the United States. Its ads and landing pages appeared to work normally, but the account was suspended after the team changed its payment method. The email suggested possible misrepresentation or misleading information.</p>
<p>During the first appeal, the team submitted a business license and a brief statement saying that the account was legitimate. The request was rejected. Before filing again, the operations team reviewed the policy notice, website, billing information, and ad assets together. It discovered that one ad described the offer as an “official collaboration tool,” while the checkout page actually sold third-party purchased accounts. The company name shown in the payment records also differed from the name in the website footer.</p>
<p>The second review revealed the real issue: Google was not simply asking whether the advertiser had a business license. It was assessing whether the information across the account was consistent and verifiable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is an anonymized scenario. Outcomes depend on the specific policy issue, evidence quality, and Google Ads review, so no appeal should be presented as guaranteed to succeed.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 1: Identify What the Review Is Actually Questioning</h2>
<p>Do not begin with a generic appeal template. First, determine four points from the email, account notices, and policy management records:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Scope of the restriction:</strong> Determine whether the entire account, a specific campaign, or only certain ads were affected.</li>
<li><strong>Policy category:</strong> Read the relevant policy, such as misrepresentation, circumventing systems, or invalid traffic. An unusual traffic issue should not be described as a false business claim.</li>
<li><strong>Timing:</strong> Review the last changes before the suspension, including the website, payment method, destination URL, tracking parameters, and ad copy.</li>
<li><strong>Submission requirements:</strong> Check Google Ads notifications, the account administrator’s inbox, and the policy management section to make sure no requested document has been overlooked.</li>
</ol>
<p>The central rule is simple: match the evidence to the exact point raised in the notice. If the reason is unclear, do not guess. State which areas were reviewed and request clarification within the appeal.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Prepare a Consistent Evidence Package</h2>
<h3>Business Identity and Account Records</h3>
<p>Provide the Ads account ID, business registration document, legal entity name, official website, and publicly verifiable business information. The legal entity on the registration document should align with the name on the payment record, website terms, and privacy policy. If the brand name differs from the legal entity, explain the ownership or authorization relationship and provide supporting records where available.</p>
<h3>Website and Landing Page Records</h3>
<p>Capture full-page screenshots that show the state of the website at the time of the appeal and include publicly accessible URLs. The evidence should cover the homepage, product page, pricing page, terms, privacy policy, contact information, and checkout page. If reviewers cannot access some material, prepare a PDF containing the relevant verifiable pages rather than submitting only a homepage screenshot.</p>
<h3>Billing and Payment Evidence</h3>
<p>Review the payment card, payment method, business payment account, and billing name. If the cardholder name differs from the registered business, provide authorization documentation issued by the bank or payment provider. Invoices, receipts, and billing screenshots may be redacted, but dates, amounts, and relevant account identifiers should remain visible.</p>
<h3>Ads and Tracking Records</h3>
<p>List each affected ad, its headline and description, the landing page, and the final destination URL. Explain whether users see the same offer advertised in the ad. Check for rewritten parameters, redirecting intermediary pages, and differences between mobile and desktop experiences. If a third-party tool is involved, explain its legitimate use rather than relying on the argument that other advertisers do the same thing.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Structure the Appeal Around Four Questions</h2>
<p>A practical format is <strong>Issue—Correction—Evidence—Commitment</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Issue:</strong> Quote the relevant policy category directly. Do not exaggerate or argue before establishing the facts.</li>
<li><strong>Correction:</strong> Explain exactly which page, billing record, or ad setting has been changed.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> List each document by title and attach the relevant URL or attachment number.</li>
<li><strong>Commitment:</strong> Describe the internal review process introduced to prevent recurrence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We identified that the wording in one ad did not accurately describe the product shown on the checkout page. We removed the statement and aligned the checkout page with the advertised offer. Please see Attachments A through D for our business registration, payment authorization, and the updated pages. Before future campaigns go live, our media and compliance teams will jointly review ad copy, destination pages, and the payment entity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do not upload unnecessary materials. Use clear file names such as “01_business_license” and “02_billing_authorization,” and include a short evidence index.</p>
<h2>Outcome and Follow-Up: Do Not Scale Immediately After Reopening</h2>
<p>In this case, the advertiser corrected the website information, aligned the payment entity, and submitted matching evidence. The account restriction was removed. The team did not immediately restore the original campaign plan. It first tested the landing page and conversion path with a controlled budget, then increased spend gradually. It also introduced review checklists for website changes, campaign launches, and payment updates.</p>
<p>If an appeal is rejected, use the new reviewer comments to identify the missing evidence rather than resubmitting the same package. Once the account is reopened, confirm whether any historical ads were permanently removed, whether billing works correctly, and whether conversion tracking has been affected. Only when the appeal record, payment account, and actual campaign delivery are aligned can the account management process be considered complete.</p>
